  "problem": "Determine the $y$-intercept of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5x-2}+1.$",
  "worked_solution": "To find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5(0)-2}+1 \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-2}+1 \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $5.$",
